Stored procedures support real control flow — conditionals, loops, error handling — inside the database engine.

```sql
CREATE PROCEDURE transfer_funds(sender INT, receiver INT, amount NUMERIC)
LANGUAGE plpgsql AS $$
BEGIN
  IF (SELECT balance FROM accounts WHERE id = sender) < amount THEN
    RAISE EXCEPTION 'insufficient funds';
  END IF;

  UPDATE accounts SET balance = balance - amount WHERE id = sender;
  UPDATE accounts SET balance = balance + amount WHERE id = receiver;
  INSERT INTO audit_log(action, detail) VALUES ('transfer', sender || '->' || receiver);
END;
$$;

CALL transfer_funds(101, 202, 50.00);
```

All three statements run in one implicit transaction — if the exception fires, nothing commits. This atomicity across multiple statements, without the client needing to manage a transaction itself, is the core reason procedures existed before application frameworks made client-side transaction handling easy.
